Q: How to hide/disable ::marker pseudo-element for safari A: <p>From what I can gather (and with limited testing), previous answers to this question either don't work (<code>::-webkit-details-marker</code>) or have the drawback of also making the list less accessible (<code>ul { list-style: none }</code> <a href="https://www.scottohara.me/blog/2019/01/12/lists-and-safari.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">1</a> <a href="https://gerardkcohen.me/writing/2017/voiceover-list-style-type.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">2</a>).</p>
<p>So, some potential options:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Remove markers with some other solution, then re-add list semantics with <code>role=&quot;list&quot;</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul role=&quot;list&quot;&gt; &lt;!-- add the list role to the &lt;ul&gt; --&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;!-- etc. --&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>See <a href="https://www.scottohara.me/blog/2019/01/12/lists-and-safari.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">article by Scott O'Hara</a> for more info.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Set font-size to 0</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>::marker { font-size: 0; }
</code></pre>
<p>This does seem to work (without removing list semantics). Tested in Safari 18.6 on a Macbook Pro with Sequoia 15.6.1.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: Regex str_replace A: <p>Here's what worked for me:</p>
<pre><code>$content=&quot;some text content with a https://www.google.com/ to click&quot;;
$regex=&quot;/https?:\/\/\S+/&quot;;
preg_match_all($regex, $content, $matches);
foreach ($matches[0] as $link){
    $content=str_replace($link, &quot;&lt;a href='&quot;.$link.&quot;'&gt;link&lt;/a&gt;&quot;, $content);
}
echo $content;
</code></pre>
<p>It turns out that if you pass a third argument to preg_match_all(), the matches will be stored in that variable as an array of arrays, of which $matches[0] is the array of 'plain' matches (which is what I'm after - $matches[1], $matches[2] etc will apparently store arrays of groups and sub-groups if the regex had them).</p>
